# KNOWN GAP — 🚨 a `&self` MUTATOR ON A GENERIC EQUIP IS A USE-AFTER-FREE.
#
# `Cell[T]` holds a `Vector[T]`; `probe(&self)` binds a view of element 0 and
# then calls `self.resize(v)`, which pushes 64 times and reallocates. The view
# dangles: rc 139 SIGSEGV.
#
# ⚠ DISTINCT FROM `t0699`, AND THE DISCRIMINATOR IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED.
# `t0699` was "a user method's NAME decides memory safety" — a name absent from
# a hand list made the CoW prescan blind. This one is NAME-INDEPENDENT: it is
# rc 139 under `resize` (a name that WAS on that list, so the prescan DID mark
# the receiver) and rc 139 under `grow` (a name that was not), before AND after
# the typed per-receiver classifier landed. The mark is made and the
# materialize still does not happen, so the defect is DOWNSTREAM of the
# classification — in the generic-equip / monomorphisation path, not in the
# prescan.
#
# ⚠ THE CONTROL IS THE POINT. Hand-monomorphise this file — `Cell[T]` -> `Cell`,
# `T` -> `String`, nothing else — and it is rc 0 printing `helloworld`, at
# stock HEAD and after the fix. ONE type parameter is the whole difference.
#
# THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING (64 pushes); one push is rc 0 and proves nothing.
# The heap payload via `mk` is NOT load-bearing for reproduction (measured on
# the sibling `t0699` shape) — it is kept so the freed element is a heap String
# and ASan reports a legible heap-use-after-free rather than a bare SIGSEGV.
#
# Related but NOT this: `todo/t0134` is the SELF-HOST lane's generic-equip gap
# in `compute_method_mutates_self`, framed there as a precision/degrade-to-BASE
# issue. This is the Rust lane, and it is a crash.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the `#[ignore]`d `generic_equip_mutator_view_uaf`):
# prints `helloworld`, both backends, ASan-clean — exactly what the
# monomorphic control already does.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Cell[T]:
    Vector[T] items

equip Cell[T]:
    void resize(&self, T v):
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            self.items.push(v)
            i = i + 1

    T probe(&self, T v):
        T s = self.items.get(0).unwrap()
        self.resize(v)
        return s

void main():
    Cell[String] h = Cell[String]([mk("hello", "world")])
    print(h.probe(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy")))
